Subject: prt_where kept across the core's calls into a module

The stock cmn_err leaves prt_where as its format's first character set
it.  One plain cmn_err in a module's _load (a "no board" notice) left
3 for the rest of the boot, and with a console logger registered every
later line went into putbuf twice; a "!" one would keep later lines off
the console.  dlm_modcall saves prt_where and puts it back around the
lifecycle calls: _load, _unload (also a failed load's), the halt entry,
a deferred call.  vfs_register's init runs inside _load.  The entry
points a module installs (cdev, STREAMS, exec, vfs operations,
interrupts) are left as they are: they are per-operation and the module
owns what they print.  The state harness's pw1 runs a plain cmn_err in
each lifecycle entry and in a deferred call.

--- a/dlm_core.c
+++ b/dlm_core.c
@@ -155,6 +155,30 @@
 		printf("%s: undefined symbol %s\n", ld->ld_name, name);
 }
 
+/*
+ * A call into a module from the core's lifecycle paths: its _load and
+ * _unload, its halt entry, a deferred call.  prt_where is put back after
+ * it: the stock cmn_err leaves prt_where as its format's first character
+ * set it, so one plain cmn_err there (a "no board" notice from a _load,
+ * say) would leave 3, and every later line would go into putbuf twice
+ * once a console logger is registered; a "!" one would keep later lines
+ * off the console.
+ */
+int
+dlm_modcall(m, f, a, b)
+	struct dlm_mod *m;
+	unsigned long f;
+	char *a;
+	long b;
+{
+	short pw = prt_where;
+	int e;
+
+	e = DLM_CALL(m, f, a, b);
+	prt_where = pw;
+	return e;
+}
